## Public-source owner checks

Cannings and Haigh, *Montreal Solitaire*, JCTA 60(1), 50--66 (1992), DOI
[10.1016/0097-3165(92)90037-U](https://doi.org/10.1016/0097-3165(92)90037-U).
Crossref metadata was actually retrieved before this intake. Direct web opens
of linkinghub and Elsevier article API were blocked; a publisher PDF HEAD
returned HTTP 403. These are access failures, not body reads. The reproducible
retrieval capsule will preserve additional actual requests and raw responses.

Discovery-only secondary pointer: Drensky's 2015 survey, arXiv 1503.00885,
[institutional PDF](https://www.math.bas.bg/smb/2015_PK/tom_2015/pdf/079-091.pdf),
section 3.4. Search extracts describe decrementing positive runs and placing
their lengths in the immediately following old zero, on an unbounded line
modulo exterior zero trimming. This is a source lead only; the primary
Cannings--Haigh theorem/proof text is NOT READ. The line/ring primitive
comparison is author inference from the explicit formulas, not an established
global conjugacy or a verified statement about the complete 1992 scope.
Primary-source coverage remains HOLD. Do not promote the low-mass result as
an advance over Montréal solitaire on the strength of this secondary lead.

The binary recolouring deduction is verified algebraically: equal neighbours
leave the centre, and unequal binary neighbours complement it, hence XOR of
all three cells. Wolfram's primary authored rule-150 notes were actually
opened at https://www.wolframscience.com/nks/notes-3-2--rule-150/ (rule formula
read). This eliminates that slice; it does not prove a ternary rule theorem.

