## Actual source-access result

`public_sources/RETRIEVAL.json` and the command/stdout/stderr/header/body files
record fresh actual requests. Crossref succeeded (2857 bytes). Elsevier's
article API returned HTTP 400, exit 22, 159 bytes; the publisher PDF URL
returned HTTP 403, exit 22, 832805 bytes of error body, not a PDF. The earlier
web-tool opens were blocked. No original Cannings--Haigh theorem/proof body
was read, and no primary-source theorem credit is claimed.

Drensky's institutional survey PDF was retrieved successfully (307191 bytes,
SHA256 `509500937de935e655bb6c812f43c5f0958f9d45337f5f0ed52a85eb8fc6d023`),
and `pdftotext -layout` exited zero. The author actually read extracted
lines 340--390 (complete section 3.4, with its adjacent context) and 480--490
(bibliographic entry 11). This remains a secondary discovery reference and
does not satisfy the missing primary-body gate. No full-survey read is claimed.

The explicit run rule there subtracts one from each positive entry and puts
the run length into its following old zero, creating an end position on an
unbounded line when needed. NED uses that primitive on a fixed labelled
ring and holds a fully occupied ring. These boundary differences are literal;
they do not prove that no known conjugacy/factor or published ring theorem
exists. The source describes reversibility for its own line model, so the
low-mass NED permutation result is particularly unsafe to market as novelty.

## Old-map adapters and limitations

The unique-two-walk relation proposal is precisely old R01/UWS: identify both
states with their Boolean adjacency matrices, including diagonal entries;
both output $(i,j)$ exactly when $\sum_k A_{ik}A_{kj}=1$. Identity intertwines
the total maps for every finite size. This is an exact collision and was not
piloted again.

For the recolouring proposal, the two-colour restriction is exactly the
three-cell XOR map, by the equal/unequal-neighbour cases. This is a full
binary-slice adapter to rule 150, not evidence for a larger-colour theorem.
No ternary carrier pilot or complete ternary proof was produced.

The old CBB carrier in `papers157_161_sequence/scouting/combinatorial/SCOUT.md`
is cyclic binary nearest-empty box--ball matching (the original ledger
lines 25--65 were actually read). NED is not identical on binary words:
$(1,1,0,0)$ maps to $(0,0,2,0)$, outside that binary carrier. Likewise, the
immediate-neighbour unit-service zero-range rule from the old CMM replacement
ledger sends this state clockwise to $(0,1,1,0)$, not the NED result. These
counterexamples rule out only those literal identity identifications; they
do not establish nonconjugacy, independence of proof mechanisms or novelty.
No additional pilot is justified by these distinctions.

